report OutputDebugStringW strings as Unicode

GDB waits for debug events with WaitForDebugEvent, so the system
converts OutputDebugStringW strings to the inferior's ANSI code page
before reporting them.  Any character outside that code page is lost
before GDB ever sees the string.

Since Windows 10, a debugger opts in to receiving these strings as
Unicode by waiting with WaitForDebugEventEx instead.  The event then
has fUnicode set, and handle_output_debug_string must read UTF-16
rather than bytes.  Reading it as bytes would stop at the zero high
byte of the first ASCII character and print only that character.

Load WaitForDebugEventEx at runtime, as with GetThreadDescription, so
GDB still runs on Windows versions that lack it, where behavior is
unchanged.  Read Unicode strings as UTF-16 and convert them to the
host charset.

The opt-in is a flag that only GDB sets.  gdbserver shares
wait_for_debug_event, and its Unicode path converts with wcstombs,
which fails on the first character the locale cannot represent, so
gdbserver keeps the old behavior.

--- a/gdb/nat/windows-nat.c
+++ b/gdb/nat/windows-nat.c
@@ -69,6 +69,14 @@
